  "output": "## Performance Analysis — Canon\n\nTwo things worth knowing before the findings: (1) `engine/` is currently **all stubs** — every pipeline/agent/db function is `raise NotImplementedError` (confirmed in `blocking.py`, `detect.py`, `normalize.py`, `graph.py`, `db.py`, `main.py`) — so there's no live query, matching, or agent logic to profile yet. (2) `web/` has no client-side state anywhere — I grepped for `useState|useEffect|useMemo|useCallback|fetch|useQuery` across `web/` and got zero matches. Every `\"use client\"` component (`nav-rail.tsx`, `landing-hero.tsx`, `pipeline-strip.tsx`, `step-list.tsx`, `get-started-hero.tsx`) uses `motion`/`usePathname` only, purely presentational. So most of the requested categories genuinely have **no current instances** — inventing them would be noise. What follows is one real finding, plus concrete guidance tied to the specific stubs that are about to get filled in per the AGENTS.md build order, so it's actionable when you get there rather than abstract.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Found now — redundant round-trip in `web/lib/server/sources.ts`\n\n```ts\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  const all = await listSources();   // SELECT * FROM sources, incl. jsonb config\n  return { crm: all.filter(...).length, warehouse: ..., synthetic: ... };\n}\n```\n\nIf any page ever needs both the list and the counts (or `countSourcesBySide` gets called from more than one route), that's two full-table round trips for something derivable from one grouped query — and it's pulling every `config` jsonb column just to count. Currently only `get-started/page.tsx` calls it standalone, so the cost is trivial today (few rows), but it's worth fixing before `sources` grows and before a second caller shows up:\n\n```ts\nimport { sql } from \"drizzle-orm\";\n\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  await connection();\n  const rows = await getDb()\n    .select({ kind: sources.kind, count: sql<number>`count(*)::int` })\n    .from(sources)\n    .groupBy(sources.kind);\n  // fold rows into { crm, warehouse, synthetic } — no full-row fetch, one query\n}\n```\n\nEverything else in `web/` (the `sources` page, `SourceTable`, landing/onboarding pages) is a single Server Component query or pure presentation — no N+1, no re-render surface, no leak candidates.\n\n---\n\n### 2. N+1 risk — flag for when `engine/db.py` and `pipeline/blocking.py` get implemented (Phase 2)\n\nThe docstrings already know the shape of the trap (`blocking.py`: *\"NEVER compare all pairs\"*), but the write side isn't guarded yet. When you implement these:\n\n```python\ndef write_entities(run_id: UUID, entities: list[CanonicalEntity]) -> None:\n    raise NotImplementedError(...)\n```\n\nthe natural first draft is a per-row `INSERT` in a loop — that's an N+1 write, and a run produces thousands of `entities`/`matches`/`conflicts` rows. Use `psycopg`'s batch execute instead:\n\n```python\nfrom psycopg import sql\nfrom psycopg.rows import dict_row\n\ndef write_entities(conn, run_id: UUID, entities: list[CanonicalEntity]) -> None:\n    with conn.cursor() as cur:\n        cur.executemany(\n            \"\"\"INSERT INTO entities (run_id, source_id, external_id, entity_type,\n                                      fields, parent_external_id, last_modified_at)\n               VALUES (%(run_id)s, %(source_id)s, %(external_id)s, %(entity_type)s,\n                       %(fields)s, %(parent_external_id)s, %(last_modified_at)s)\"\"\",\n            [{\"run_id\": run_id, **e.model_dump()} for e in entities],\n        )\n```\n\nSame applies to `write_matches` / `write_conflicts` / `write_resolutions`.\n\nOn the read side, `pipeline/detect.py` already takes the right shape — `entities: dict[UUID, CanonicalEntity]` preloaded once, not looked up per-match — keep that pattern when you implement it; don't let it regress into a per-conflict DB lookup.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Redundant computation + caching — `pipeline/normalize.py`, `pipeline/blocking.py`, `agent/prompts.py`\n\n- **`fold_name`**: org hierarchies repeat names heavily (same territory/company suffix across hundreds of accounts). Memoize it — it's a pure, deterministic function per the docstring's own contract:\n  ```python\n  from functools import lru_cache\n\n  @lru_cache(maxsize=None)\n  def fold_name(name: str) -> str:\n      ...\n  ```\n\n- **Embeddings in `blocking.py`**: `sentence-transformers` should batch-encode (`model.encode(list_of_names)`), not one entity at a time — one-by-one encoding throws away most of the throughput benefit of a transformer batch and will dominate runtime on a 500+ account org.\n\n- **The model/FAISS index itself**: load once as a module-level singleton, not per-run or per-request:\n  ```python\n  _MODEL: SentenceTransformer | None = None\n\n  def get_model() -> SentenceTransformer:\n      global _MODEL\n      if _MODEL is None:\n          _MODEL = SentenceTransformer(\"all-MiniLM-L6-v2\")\n      return _MODEL\n  ```\n  Reloading a transformer per run is the realistic \"memory leak\" shape here — not an actual leak, but repeated allocation/deallocation of a multi-hundred-MB model under `BackgroundTasks`, which will fragment memory and stall the FastAPI event loop since `BackgroundTasks` runs in-process. Worth running the embedding/matching stage in a thread pool executor (`run_in_executor`) once it exists, so a long CPU-bound run doesn't block `/runs/:id/status` polling.\n\n- **Prompt caching (`agent/prompts.py`)**: already flagged in the file's own docstring as \"the single largest cost lever\" — when you implement `build_ruleset_block`, set `cache_control` on that block explicitly:\n  ```python\n  {\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": ruleset_block, \"cache_control\": {\"type\": \"ephemeral\"}}\n  ```\n  and make sure `build_conflict_block` output is appended *after* it, never interleaved — interleaving breaks the cache prefix match silently.\n\n---\n\n### 4. React re-renders / leaks — nothing now; the one place to watch is Phase 4\n\nThere's no conflict queue, run-status polling, or client state yet, so there's nothing to profile. When you build it per AGENTS.md (TanStack Query for run-status polling, the `j/k/a/e/o`-navigable conflict queue \"a reviewer working a queue of 400\"):\n\n- Memoize `ConflictDiffRow` (`React.memo`) and keep row-level handlers stable (`useCallback` keyed by conflict id, not inline closures recreated every parent render) — otherwise every poll tick re-renders all 400 rows.\n- Scope TanStack Query's `refetchInterval` to non-terminal run statuses only, and let it go `false` once `status === \"complete\" | \"failed\"` — an unbounded interval poll is the standard leak/waste pattern to avoid:\n  ```ts\n  useQuery({\n    queryKey: [\"run\", runId, \"status\"],\n    queryFn: () => fetchRunStatus(runId),\n    refetchInterval: (query) =>\n      query.state.data?.status === \"complete\" || query.state.data?.status === \"failed\"\n        ? false\n        : 2000,\n  });\n  ```\n- A queue of 400 rows with keyboard nav is a virtualization candidate (windowing) rather than rendering all rows — worth deciding on before building it, not after.\n\n---\n\n**Bottom line**: one small real fix (§1) you can make now; everything else is guidance to apply *when* the corresponding stub in `engine/` or the conflict queue in `web/` gets implemented, not a problem in the codebase today.\n",
